\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szappend\par
\cf5     Append string data to a buffer and return the next append location\par
\cf1\par
    \cf3 FORM\cf1\par
\cf2     szappend proc buffer:\cf6 QWORD\cf2 ,addstr:\cf6 QWORD\cf2 ,location:\cf6 QWORD\cf2\par
\cf1\par
    \cf3 ARGUMENTS\cf1\par
\cf2     1. buffer   \cf5 The address of the buffer to append data to\par
\cf1\par
\cf2     2. addstr\cf1    \cf5 The address of the string to append to it\par
\cf1\par
\cf2     3. location\cf1  \cf5 The current location counter\par
\cf1\par
\cf3     RETURN VALUE\par
\cf5     The end location plus \cf2 1 BYTE\cf5  of the last append operation. This is\par
    used as the next write position. It also serves as the final length\par
    of the appended data so it can be used to truncate memory if required.\par
\cf1\par
    \cf3 EXAMPLE\cf1\par
        LOCAL cloc  :QWORD                      \cf7 ; the current location counter\cf1\par
        mov cloc, 0                             \cf7 ; zero the counter\par
\cf1\par
      \cf7 ; loop code\par
\cf1\par
\cf2         mov cloc, rv(szappend,pAddStr,cloc)     \cf7 ; append another string each iteration\par
                                                ; of the loop code while updating \cf2 "cloc"\cf1\par
\cf2       \cf7 ; end loop code\par
\par
\cf3     NOTES\par
\cf5     This algorithm requires a location counter to be set before it\par
    is called. It would usually be set to \cf2 zero (0)\cf5 . After each append\par
    operation the return value is the write location for the next\par
    append operation. The buffer to append to must be large enough to\par
    hold all appended data.\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szCatStr\par
\cf5     Peform a simple string append to the end of an existing string buffer\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     FORM\par
\cf2     szCatStr proc src:\cf6 QWORD\cf2 , lpAdd:\cf6 QWORD\cf2\par
\par
\cf3     ARGUMENTS\par
\cf2     1. src      \cf5 The address of the existing string to append another string to.\par
\par
\cf2     2. lpAdd    \cf5 The address of the string to append to the first string.\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     NOTES\par
\cf6     The source buffer must be large enough to hold the appended string data.\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szCmp\par
\cf5     Case sensitive comparison of two \cf2 (2)\cf5  strings\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     ARGUMENTS\par
\cf2     1. \cf5 The address of the first string\par
\cf2\par
    2. \cf5 The address of the second string\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     RETURN VALUE\par
\cf5     If there is no match, the return value in rax = \cf2 0\cf5 .\par
    If there is a match, the return value in rax is \cf2 NON zero\cf5 .\par
\par
\cf3     COMMENTS\par
\cf6     Test the result using code similar to this.\par
\cf5\par
\cf2         test rax, rax                           \cf7 ; test for zero\par
\cf2         jz nomatch                              \cf7 : branch on no match\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szCmpi\par
\cf5     Case insensitive comparison of two \cf2 (2)\cf5  strings\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     ARGUMENTS\par
\cf2     1. \cf5 The address of the first string\par
\cf2\par
    2. \cf5 The address of the second string\par
\par
\cf3     RETURN VALUE\par
\cf5     If there is no match, the return value in rax = \cf2 0\cf5 .\par
    If there is a match, the return value in rax is \cf2 NON zero\cf5 .\par
\par
\cf3     COMMENTS\par
\cf5     Test the result using code similar to this.\par
\par
\cf2         test rax, rax                           \cf7 ; test for zero\par
\cf2         jz nomatch                              \cf7 : branch on no match\par
\par
\cf3     NOTES\par
\cf5     The algorithm uses a \cf2 256\cf5  character lookup table that is for the English language. If you need\par
    to perform case insensitive string comparisons in a language that does not use the same character\par
    set as international English, you need to copy this algorithm with a different name and change\par
    the lookup table at the beginning of the source. This can be done using the \cf2 MASM32/64\cf5  utility\par
    \cf2 "maketbl.exe"\cf5 .\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szCopy\par
\cf5     Copy a zero terminated string to another buffer\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     ARGUMENTS\par
\cf2     1. \cf5 The address of the string to copy\par
\cf2\par
    2. \cf5 The address of the buffer to copy it to\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     RETURN VALUE\par
\cf5     Return value is the address of the target buffer.\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     COMMENTS\par
\cf6     The target buffer must be large enough to hold the data copied to it.\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szLeft\par
\cf5     Copy a user defined number of characters from the left side of a zero terminated string to a\par
    user defined b8uffer\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     ARGUMENTS\par
\cf2     1. \cf5 The address of the source string to copy from\par
\cf2\par
    2. \cf5 The destination buffer to copy the characters to\par
\cf2\par
    3. \cf5 The number of characters to copy\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     COMMENTS\par
\cf5     The target buffer must be large enough to hold the data copied to it. It is safe to set a\par
    buffer of the same size as the source buffer in the event that the required characters count\par
    is as long as the source buffer.\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szRight\par
\cf5     Get a user defined number of characters from the right side of a zero terminated string\par
\cf2\par
    \cf3 ARGUMENTS\cf2\par
    1. \cf5 The address of the string to obtain characters from\par
\cf2\par
    2. \cf5 The address of the output buffer\par
\cf2\par
    3. \cf5 The number of characters to obtain\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     RETURN VALUE\par
\cf6     The return value is the destination address.\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
