\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szRtrim\par
\cf5     Trim tabs and spaces from the right side of a string\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     ARGUMENTS\par
\cf2     1. \cf5 The address of the source string\par
\cf2\par
    2. \cf5 The address of the destination buffer\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     RETURN VALUE\par
\cf6     The return value is the destination address.\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szTrim\par
\cf5     Trim tabs and space from both ends of a string\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     ARGUMENTS\par
\cf2     1. \cf5 The address of the string to trim\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     NOTE\par
\cf6     This algorithm overwrites the original string.\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szMono\par
\cf5     This algorithm trim left and right sides of a string, replaces tabs with spaces and resticts\par
    spacing to single space\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     ARGUMENTS\par
\cf2     1. \cf5 The address of the string to clean up\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     RETURN VALUE\par
\cf5     The return value is the original address of the string.\par
\par
\cf3     NOTE\par
\cf6     The original string is overwritten with the result.\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szmulticat\par
\cf5     This algorithm is designed to append multiple strings to a buffer large enough to hold all of\par
    the data being written to it. It is intended to be called from a macro that handles the\par
    complexities of a variable argument list which may exceed the first 4 registers defined in\par
    the Win 64 ABI. \cf2 It is \cf8\ul not recommended\cf2\ulnone  that it be used directly.\par
\par
\cf3     ARGUMENTS\par
\cf2     1. \cf5 Target buffer address\par
\cf2\par
    2. \cf5 The argument count\par
\cf2\par
    3. \cf5 The variable argument list from address \cf2 [rbp+32]\cf5  upwards\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     NOTE\par
\cf5     The algorithm repeatedly calls the \cf2 "szappend"\cf5  algorithm to append each string in the argument\par
    list to the target buffer.\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szRemove\par
\cf5     Remove all instances of a user defined string in a source string\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     ARGUMENTS\par
\cf2     1. \cf5 The address of the string to remove data from\par
\cf2\par
    2. \cf5 The destination buffer to write the results to\par
\cf2\par
    3. \cf5 The address of the string to remove from the source buffer\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     FORM\par
\cf2     szRemove proc src:\cf6 QWORD\cf2 ,dst:\cf6 QWORD\cf2 ,remv:\cf6 QWORD\cf2\par
\par
\cf3     RETURN VALUE\par
\cf5     The return value is the destination address.\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     NOTE\par
\cf5     If you do not need to preserve the original string, you may use the source address as the\par
    target address which will overwrite the original string.\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szRep\par
\cf5     Replace existing text in a source string with user defined replacement strings. \cf8\ul Be sure you\par
\ulnone     \ul read the notes below\cf5\ulnone  as this form of replacement is not a simple task if the replacement is\par
    longer than the text it replaces.\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     ARGUMENTS\par
\cf2     1. \cf5 The address of the text to perform replacements in\par
\cf2\par
    2. \cf5 The destination address of the modified string\par
\cf2\par
    3. \cf5 The address of the text to replace\par
\cf2\par
    4. \cf5 The address of the text to replace it with\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     FORM\par
\cf2     szRep proc src:\cf6 QWORD\cf2 ,dst:\cf6 QWORD\cf2 ,txt1:\cf6 QWORD\cf2 ,txt2:\cf6 QWORD\cf2\par
\par
\cf3     NOTES\par
\cf5     In the simple sense, if only small amounts of text need to be replaced, you can take a short\par
    cut and just allocate a buffer that is big enough to hold any results.\par
\cf2\par
\cf6     If for example the string to perform replacements in is only 100 bytes or less, then allocating\par
    \cf2 1k\cf6  can be done on the stack as a LOCAL in safety. You can adjust this if the replacements are\par
    a lot larger than the text to replace.\par
\par
\cf5     The complicating factor is if the replacement is longer than the text it is to replace. If the\par
    replacement is the same length or shorter then you can safely allocate a buffer the some length\par
    as the original string.\par
\cf6\par
    If the replacement in longer, the \cf2 "szWcnt"\cf6  algorithm is useful in determining the number of\par
    instances of the string to replace in the source string. Then do the simple arithmetic to\par
    calculate how much longer the output buffer needs to be to hold the results.\par
\cf2\par
\cf5     Of the two options, that latter with calculated memory requirements is the safest and scales\par
    up to the limit of available memory.\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
\cf2   szRev\par
\cf5     Reverse the characters in a zero terminated string\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     ARGUMENTS\par
\cf2     1. \cf5 The source address of the string to reverse\par
\cf2\par
    2. \cf5 The destination address of the reversed string\par
\cf2\par
\cf3     NOTE\par
\cf6     If you do not need to preserve the original string you can use the same address for both\par
    source and destination.\par
\cf4   __________________________________________________________________________________________________\par
  \'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\'af\par
